{"name":"Gnome Green","hex":"#adc484","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gnome","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elemental (spirit or corporeal creature associated with a classical element) associated with earth.","One of a race of imaginary human-like beings, usually depicted as short and typically bearded males, who inhabit the inner parts of the earth and act as guardians of mines, mineral treasure, etc.; in modern fantasy literature and games, when distinguished from dwarves, gnomes are usually smaller than dwarves and more focused on engineering than mining."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A brief reflection or maxim; a pithy saying."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gnome"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gnome","blocks":[{"text":"A gnome is a mythological creature and diminutive spirit in Renaissance magic and alchemy, introduced by the Swiss physician and alchemist Paracelsus in the 16th century, and widely adopted by authors, including those of modern fantasy literature. They are typically depicted as small humanoids who live underground. Gnome characteristics are reinterpreted to suit various storytellers and artists."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Heinrich_Schlitt_Gnom_mit_Zeitung_und_Tabakspfeife.jpg/330px-Heinrich_Schlitt_Gnom_mit_Zeitung_und_Tabakspfeife.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnome","wikidata":"Q188215"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Olaus%281555%29-Hist_septentrionalibus-p210-demons-in-mines.jpg/500px-Olaus%281555%29-Hist_septentrionalibus-p210-demons-in-mines.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Olaus%281555%29-Hist_septentrionalibus-p210-demons-in-mines.jpg/500px-Olaus%281555%29-Hist_septentrionalibus-p210-demons-in-mines.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Demons in the mine. Olaus Magnus, Historia de gentibus septentrionalibus[87][77]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOlaus(1555)-Hist_septentrionalibus-p210-demons-in-mines.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Praetorius%281668%29-p0000-02Bergm%C3%A4nnerlein_Wic